<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Fix for GEM+ 05.xml's flagman limit. Apply right after 05.xml.

  05.xml raises GPL's flagman limit from 55 to 127 by moving two arrays of the flagman
  manager: the SUBOBJECTs to +0xEEA and the XFORMs they hang from to +0x10E6
  (gpl_trackBindFlagmen 0x481C73 / 0x481C79, the unbind's base at 0x481DF5). It leaves the
  unbind, gpl_flagmanUnbindAll 0x481DE0, reading each XFORM at the stock distance from its
  SUBOBJECT:

      0x481E0B  8B 86 DC 00 00 00   mov eax, [esi+0DCh]
      0x481E21  8B 86 DC 00 00 00   mov eax, [esi+0DCh]

  so XFORM i is taken from SUBOBJECT slot 55 + i. Those slots are 0 until a track with more
  than 55 flagmen fills them (Isle of Man has 79); from then on, leaving any track writes
  through that track's freed pointers: a crash at 0x481E29 leaving monza10k after Isle of
  Man (dump of 2026-10-25, GPLVR report 105 section 4.14). It also means the unbind never
  restored any XFORM's child before.

  Both displacements become 0x10E6 - 0xEEA = 0x1FC. The unbind runs before the track object
  is freed (sub_4A58E0), so the restoring writes land in the live track.

  Only for an exe built with 05.xml: on a stock exe the arrays are 0xDC apart and these
  bytes are already right.
-->
<GPLPatch>

  <BinaryPatch>
    <VirtualAddress>0x00481E0D</VirtualAddress>
    <Data>fc010000</Data>
  </BinaryPatch>

  <BinaryPatch>
    <VirtualAddress>0x00481E23</VirtualAddress>
    <Data>fc010000</Data>
  </BinaryPatch>

</GPLPatch>
